# gitleaks — allowlisted findings, by exact fingerprint.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 🔴 WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: Security CI HAD BEEN RED FOR A WEEK AND NOBODY SAW IT.
#
# `gitleaks/gitleaks-action@v2` does NOT scan the same thing on every trigger.
# On `push` it scans only the commits in that push; on `schedule` and
# `workflow_dispatch` it scans the ENTIRE history. So a finding that lives in an
# older commit is INVISIBLE to every push run and fires only on the Monday cron.
#
# Measured, not assumed — the same commit `4188c2a`:
#   run 31345481843  push      2026-08-10 00:49  SUCCESS
#   run 31358003733  schedule  2026-08-10 05:15  FAILURE
# Scheduled runs: 2026-07-27 pass, 2026-08-03 FAIL, 2026-08-10 FAIL. Both
# findings below predate 08-03, and eight green push runs in between said
# nothing. **A gate whose passing and whose not-running look identical is the
# defect class this repository has paid for most** — here it was worse, because
# the gate WAS running and its red was landing where nobody looked.
#
# ── ⚠️ FINGERPRINTS, NOT PATH RULES ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The obvious fix is an allowlist regex over `reviews/**.md` or over spec files.
# It was rejected. A review log is generated from whatever the reviewer read, so
# a path rule there would silently swallow a REAL credential the day one is
# quoted into it — and a secrets scanner that cannot fail is worse than none.
# A fingerprint is `commit:path:rule:line`: it pins ONE line of ONE file in ONE
# commit. Anything else, anywhere else, still fires.
#
# ── EACH ENTRY MUST BE ARGUED, NOT ASSERTED ─────────────────────────────────
#
# Both entries below are the SAME synthetic token, checked by decoding it rather
# than by trusting its filename:
#   header  {"alg":"HS256"}     payload {"sub":"123456"}
#   signature  base64("AliceSignature")
# It is a fixture with no issuer, no key and no realm — it authenticates nothing
# anywhere. It must stay JWT-SHAPED: the test it lives in proves `safeMessage`
# redacts a bare JWT, so replacing it with an obviously-fake string would delete
# the coverage rather than the finding.
#
# 1. redaction.spec.ts:58 — where the fixture is defined.
# 2. codex-security-review.md:3666 — the same line, QUOTED BACK in a Codex
#    review log that embedded the file listing it read. Not a second secret.
#
# Verified with `git blame -L <line>,<line>` against both commits; each blames to
# exactly the commit gitleaks named.
#
# ▶ IF A THIRD ENTRY EVER WANTS ADDING: decode it first. If it decodes to
#   anything with a real issuer, audience or key id, it is a leak — rotate it,
#   do not list it here.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

